# Network Threat Hunting & Protocol Analysis Automation Lab

An automated network threat hunting engine built in Python using **TShark** and **Scapy**. This tool analyzes packet capture (`.pcap`) files to identify suspicious Active Directory, SMB, NTLM, and Service Control Manager protocol activity, outputting structured SIEM-ready JSON logs for security analysts.

---

## Technical Overview

Modern network attackers often leverage administrative tools (e.g., PsExec, SMB share access) or target legacy authentication protocols (e.g., NTLMSSP downgrade vectors). Standard Wireshark display filters can miss raw socket-level payloads if protocols aren't strictly framed, while pure byte-searching lacks protocol-level context.

This tool employs a **Dual-Engine Detection Strategy**:
1. **TShark Protocol Dissection Engine:** Performs fast, field-accurate extraction of dissected SMB2 and NTLMSSP attributes (e.g., `smb2.tree.name`, `ntlmssp`).
2. **Scapy Fallback & Raw Byte Inspection Engine:** Scans raw TCP stream payloads directly to catch obfuscated, malformed, or un-dissected protocol indicators (`C$`, `ADMIN$`, `IPC$`, `svcctl`, `PSEXESVC`, `NTLMSSP`).


                       [ Packet Capture (.pcap) ]
                                      │
                 ┌────────────────────┴────────────────────┐
                 ▼                                         ▼
     [ TShark Dissection Engine ]              [ Scapy Raw Stream Engine ]
     - SMB2 Tree Access Filters               - Stream-level Byte Matching
     - Dissected NTLMSSP Fields               - Raw Pipe & Share Extraction
                 │                                         │
                 └────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                                      ▼
                           [ Deduplication Engine ]
                                      │
                 ┌────────────────────┴────────────────────┐
                 ▼                                         ▼
        [ CLI Alert Summary ]                   [ SIEM-Ready JSON Export ]

        ---

## Threat Detection & MITRE ATT&CK Mapping

| Threat / Activity | Severity | Protocol Indicator | MITRE ATT&CK Technique |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Admin Share Access** | `HIGH` | `C$`, `ADMIN$`, `IPC$` | [T1021.002 - SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002/) |
| **PsExec / Service Control** | `CRITICAL` | `svcctl`, `PSEXESVC` | [T1569.002 - Service Execution](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1569/002/) |
| **NTLM Downgrade / Relay** | `HIGH` | `NTLMSSP` Exchange | [T1557.001 - LLMNR/NBT-NS & NTLM Relay](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1557/001/) |

---

## Directory Structure

```text
network-threat-hunting-lab/
├── automation-scripts/
│   ├── analyze_smb_kerberos.py   # Core Python threat hunting engine
│   ├── ntlm_attack.pcap          # Synthetic test capture (NTLMSSP vector)
│   ├── psexec_attack.pcap        # Synthetic test capture (PsExec named pipe)
│   ├── smb_admin_attack.pcap     # Synthetic test capture (Admin share access)
│   └── ntlm_alerts.json          # Exported SIEM JSON alert output
└── README.md

Prerequisites & Installation
Requirements
Python 3.8+

Wireshark / TShark (Must be in your system $PATH)

Python Dependencies
Install Scapy via pip:
Bash
pip install scapy

Usage
Run the analysis script against any .pcap capture file. Use the --json flag to export structured logs.

Basic Execution
Bash
python automation-scripts/analyze_smb_kerberos.py automation-scripts/ntlm_attack.pcap

Export to SIEM-Ready JSON
python automation-scripts/analyze_smb_kerberos.py automation-scripts/ntlm_attack.pcap --json automation-scripts/ntlm_alerts.json

Sample Outputs
Terminal Console Output
======================================================================
[*] Starting Threat Hunting Analysis: ntlm_attack.pcap
======================================================================

[✔] Processed SMB2 & Kerberos activity.
[✔] Total Security Alerts Identified: 1

PKT #    | SEVERITY   | ALERT TYPE                               | SRC IP          -> DST IP
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1        | HIGH       | NTLM Authentication Over SMB (Downgrade Hazard) | 10.0.0.50       -> 10.0.0.5
         └─ Details: Identified NTLMSSP exchange over SMB. Potential NTLM Relay or credential harvesting vector.

[+] Successfully exported SIEM-ready JSON output to: ntlm_alerts.json

3. Save the file as **`README.md`** directly inside the root folder (`network-threat-hunting-lab/`).

